From: lefty@apple.com (Lefty)
Subject: Earth Day

>> That is, by what criteria do you label somebody an "environmentalist"?
>> And it can't be some of the same criteria you use to label somebody
>> a "leftist", or else the conclusion that environmentalists are leftists
>> is a tautology.
>
>Nevertheless, the word is almost universally understood to refer to
>those of leftward persuasion.

This, then, is a fundamental problem.  If, as Eli says, "environmentalism"
is inextricably associated with "leftism" or "collectivism", then the word
does not mean what it seems to; it is, in fact, a weasel word for "commie
pinko neophobe".

>I am technically an `environmentalist', in
>that I share many of the ultimate goals associated with that word.
>I suspect most people are.

I sympathize with Derek and Eli here.  I sort out my cans and bottles, I
compost my kitchen wastes, I would gleefully perforate anyone who tried to
damage one of _my_ redwood trees.

Am I an environmentalist?  Ray seems to think so, without knowing even this
much about me.

Am I a "leftist"?  Not that I'm aware of.  Nor am I a member of Greenpeace,
Earth First, the Sierra Club or the Audobon Society.

However, I have little sympathy with, for instance, Perry's stated
willingness to decimate the number of species on the planet if that's what
it takes to guarantee him his choice of frozen pizzas.

Here's the problem I see in Ray's posting: sympathy for general goals does
not imply sympathy for specific ones, particularly specific _extremist_
ones.  Ray makes statements like "environmentalists want everyone to use
public transit and live in compact-urban cities" without regard for the
fact that there is clearly a range of degrees of environmental concern, as
well as a range of proposed solutions.  To paint "environmentalists" as
some kind of monolithic block does no one a service.

